{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[2],"question":"On which dataset do the authors show their AMR parser is competitive with the state of the art?","answer":"The LDC2015E86 dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is the name of the new transition system introduced in the paper, and what earlier system inspired it?","answer":"The new system is called AmrEager, and it was inspired by the ArcEager dependency transition system of nivre2004."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[3],"question":"In which two areas does the proposed parser outperform state-of-the-art AMR parsers?","answer":"It outperforms state-of-the-art parsers for recovering named entities and handling polarity."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How does the CAMR parser (wang2boosting) differ from the authors' parser in processing order?","answer":"CAMR first acquires the entire dependency tree and then processes it bottom-up, whereas the authors' parser processes the sentence left-to-right."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the two most common transition systems used for greedy dependency parsing?","answer":"ArcStandard and ArcEager."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What traditional NLP tasks does Abstract Meaning Representation (AMR) embed annotations for?","answer":"AMR embeds annotations for named entity recognition, semantic role labeling, word sense disambiguation, and co-reference resolution."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0070","fact_refs":[8],"question":"In the ArcEager transition system, how are left arcs versus right arcs constructed?","answer":"Left arcs are constructed bottom-up and right arcs are constructed top-down."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0071","statement":"The paper defines its initial goal as the listening comprehension test of TOEFL, a challenging academic English examination for non-native English speakers.","source_span":"We define the initial goal as the listening comprehension test of TOEFL, a challenging academic English examination for English learners whose native language is not English."}
